{
 "id": "self_promotion",
 "version": "1.3.0",
 "depends_on": [
  "gap_awareness"
 ],
 "description": "SELF-PROMOTION (tranche 20, at the user's direction: 'the agent should be able to do its own promotion'). Until now a self-filed SelfConcern became a StandingConcern, and so reached the experiment pipeline, only when a HUMAN asked: a steer turn, whose steer_promote_concern rule set promote_requested, or the UI. This seed lets the agent request its own promotion. `self_promote_noted_concern` marks promote_requested=1.0 on the OLDEST noted SelfConcern whose kind is in SelfPromotionPolicy.eligible_kinds. It does so only when the loop is idle: no StandingConcern pending, no Experiment requested or running without a verdict, no other promotion requested, and no retry requested. Each concern is self-promoted at most once (self_promote_attempted), and the policy's own budget caps the total (promoted_count < max_promotions). The daemon's existing steer hook (jabberwock_daemon._steer_tick, scheduled by cognitive_scheduler whenever a promotion is requested) performs the promotion, exactly as for a human steer. The graph decides; Python executes. Safety is unchanged downstream: every self-change still passes the dispatched replay judge, the adoption safety veto (adoption_safety), the provenance denylist, and improvement_integrity (tranche 19: the loop cannot change its own machinery, and this rule names SelfConcern, so it is itself protected).",
 "nodes": [
  {
   "local_id": 0,
   "type": "SelfPromotionPolicy",
   "attrs": {
    "name": "self_promotion",
    "enabled": 1,
    "eligible_kinds": [
     "instruction",
     "comprehension",
     "unanswered",
     "reply_quality"
    ],
    "max_promotions": 1000,
    "promoted_count": 0,
    "description": "Agent-held policy: which of its own concern kinds it may promote without a human, and how many in total. v1.1.0 default: kinds whose promotion takes the CONVERSATION replay pipeline with an exact judge. Excluded after the tranche-20 v1 run: 'self_improvement' (its ARC bundle mints a world-game Experiment that cannot dispatch when no world is attached; measured: it held the loop busy for the rest of the stream) and 'performance' (its judge compares wall-clock mean turn latency without a quantised grain, so a variant can be 'supported' by timing noise; measured: it adopted an ablation of reply_held_capability, a rule that never fired in the replay). Knowledge gaps stay on the comprehend-first learning path. v1.2.0 (tranche 22): 'unanswered' -- the agent's own recurring unanswered turn class (seeds/unanswered_gap.json), judged by the production-path conversation replay. v1.3.0 (plan P2): 'reply_quality' -- the agent's own recurring bad-reply class (seeds/reply_feedback_gap.json), enabled only now that judge v2 (adoption_safety v1.2.0) refuses fixes that lower reply quality."
   }
  }
 ],
